          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong Predicative mathematics
            is a way of doing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            without allowing impredicative definitions.
          %p
            Informally, a definition is
            %em impredicative
            if it refers to a totality which includes the thing being defined. For example, the definition of a particular real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as the least upper bound of a given set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is impredicative, because it characterizes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as a particular element of some set (the upper bounds of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            can be defined in some other way and then proved to be the least upper bound of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            There are (at least) two broad schools of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations"} foundations
            of predicative mathematics that don't talk much to each other: one school that uses lower-order forms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher-order+logic"} higher-order logic
            (or the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            that this justifies) and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+logic"} classical logic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+mathematics"} constructive
            school that uses first-order
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            (or the set theory that this justifies) and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+logic"} intuitionistic logic
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} the category of sets
            to be.
          %p
            Constructive predicativists sometimes accept principles (such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            and the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed "." do
              %em weakly predicative
          %h3#power_classes_vs_no_power_sets_at_all Power classes vs no power sets at all
          %p
            Another division within predicative mathematics is whether
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            can be formed but are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+classes"} proper classes
            instead of sets or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+sets"} large sets
            instead of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+sets"} small sets
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            simply cannot be formed at all.
          %p
            In the former case, almost everything which could be done in impredicative mathematics could be done in predicative mathematics, only with the requirement that size issues have to be taken into consideration at all times. This is the case with predicative constructive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theories"} dependent type theories
            which have
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            one could construct a large
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+propositions"} type of propositions
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega \coloneqq \sum_{A:U} \mathrm{isProp}(A)
            with power sets as function sets with codomain
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class+theory"} class theory
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morse-Kelley+class+theory"} Morse-Kelley class theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+classes"} power classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_n(S)
            for hierarchy level
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            with a cumulative hierarchy of universes, with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_{n + 1}(S)
            larger than
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}_n(S)
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Russell"} Bertrand Russell
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Principia+Mathematica"} Principia Mathematica
          %p
            When power sets don’t exist at all, whether as a set or a proper class, this results in significantly weaker foundations, since in this case one simply cannot form various mathematical structures which require the use of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+real+numbers"} Dedekind real numbers
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frames"} frames
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locales"} locales
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+pretopos"} Heyting
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+pretopos"} Boolean pretopos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theories"} material set theories
            like
            %span.newWikiWord
              Kripke–Platek set theory
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Kripke%E2%80%93Platek+set+theory"} ?
            which do not have an internal notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            in the type theory itself, since otherwise
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isProp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{A:U} \mathrm{isProp}(A)
            is a large
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+propositions"} type of propositions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            results in additional structure like
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+topologies"} formal topologies
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+covers"} inductive covers
            not being definable in the type theory, since without universes or types of propositions one cannot define relations between elements and subtypes.
          %h2#impredicative_axioms Impredicative axioms
          %p Not all of these axioms are rejected by all predicativists, but they at least come under some suspicion.
          %h3#infinity Infinity
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
            is not usually considered impredicative, but we list it anyway, as it is needed for the others to have force. Mathematics that does not require this axiom,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+mathematics"} finite mathematics
          %h3#power_set Power set
          %p
            The axiom that any set has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+set"} power set
            is perhaps the fundamental feature missing from predicative mathematics. In particular, the sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}, \mathcal{P}\mathbb{N}, \mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}\mathbb{N}, \ldots
          %p may be accepted in part, but not forever.
          %p
            The failure of the power set axiom means that the category of sets is not an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
          %h3#unbounded_separation Unbounded separation
          %p
            The constructive school generally accepts the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+replacement"} axiom of replacement
            but not unbounded forms of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+separation"} axiom of separation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            together imply full separation.)
          %p
            Naïvely, the axiom of separation says that, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a set and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            to the set of
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{ A | P \} = \{ x \in A \;|\; P(x) \} .
          %p
            To be precise, however, this
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            should be written as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate"} predicate
            in the language of set theory. The form of separation justified by type theory and such structural set theories as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            requires the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantifiers"} quantifiers
            in this predicate to be guarded by sets; unbounded separation is the generalisation of this to arbitrary quantifiers.
          %p
            Arguably, the impredicative core of both separation and power sets (in the presence of bounded separation) is
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limited+separation"} limited separation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            may be guarded by power classes.
          %p
            %em We need more on this, particularly with regards to the classical school and replacement.
          %h3#function_sets Function sets
          %p
            One sometimes speaks of forbidding
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi ℕ
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℕ
                        %mi ℕ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}, \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}, \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}}, \ldots
          %p that is avoided.
          %p
            Of course, function sets can be constructed out of power sets (using
            %em bounded
            separation), so forbidding function sets certainly forbids power sets. The converse holds if there is a set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truth+value"} truth value
          %p
            With
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1\}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
          %p
            The constructive school, however, often accepts function sets (thus being
            %em weakly
            predicative). In this school, the sequence above is fine. Actually, the slightly stronger axiom of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset+collection"} subset collection
            is adopted by
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Aczel"} Peter Aczel
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CZF
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CZF
            and justified by
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Per+Martin-L%C3%B6f"} Per Martin-Löf
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ITT
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ITT
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brouwer"}> Brouwer
            , on the other hand, did not accept the sequence above, although his followers differ on when (if ever) it stops.
          %h3#bijection_sets Bijection sets
          %p
            In the presence of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection+sets"} bijection sets
            are in bijection with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
          %h3#univalence_axiom Univalence axiom
          %p
            One consequence of the rejection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection+sets"} bijection sets
            in predicative mathematics with the axiom of choice is that the univalence axiom of a universe of sets is no longer available, since it postulates a bijection between equality and bijection sets.
          %h3#excluded_middle Excluded middle
          %p
            Classical predicativists of course accept
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructivism"} constructivists
          %h3#the_axiom_of_choice The axiom of choice
          %p
            Some classical predicativists accept the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
          %h3#propositional_resizing Propositional resizing
          %p
            In the constructive school, one would sometimes have multiple sets of propositions, but those only represent the set of
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingletons"} subsingletons
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_U
            is equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_V
          %p
            The axiom of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+resizing"} propositional resizing
            is then statement that given any two universes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_U
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ω
                    %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_V
            of propositions in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            respectively are in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            with each other
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi U
                    %mo ≅
                    %msub
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega_U \cong \Omega_V
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+sets"} function sets
            imply
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
          %h3#illfounded_structures Ill-founded structures
          %p
            Most foundations of mathematics are predicative in one sense: no set may belong to itself. This (or rather, a certain strengthening of this) is the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+foundation"} axiom of foundation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo •
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet = \{\bullet\}
            %em define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bullet
            using antifoundation, yet this is about as impredicative as a definition can get.
          %p
            Once one accepts the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+infinity"} axiom of infinity
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive-inductive+types"} inductive-inductive types
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+inductive+types"} quotient inductive types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/initial+algebra"} initial algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinductive+type"} Coinductive types
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra"} final coalgebra
          %h3#propositional_impredicativity Propositional impredicativity
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+propositional+impredicativity"} axiom of propositional impredicativity
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            says that given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsingletons"} subsingletons
            is
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            are typically
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed"} cartesian closed
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed"} locally cartesian closed
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            = succeed "-small" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sets"} category of sets
            in a universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Set
                    %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Set}_U
            is always an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+topos"} elementary topos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+impredicativity"} propositional impredicativity
            for more details.
          %h3#impredicative_polymorphism Impredicative polymorphism
          %p
            A different sort of impredicativity, called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+polymorphism"} impredicative polymorphism
            is to be found in some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            or a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi Type
                    %mo ,
                    %mi α
                    %mo →
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall \alpha:Type, \alpha\to\alpha
            %em every
            type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∀
                  %mi α
                  %mo :
                  %mi Type
                  %mo ,
                  %mi α
                  %mo →
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall \alpha:Type, \alpha\to\alpha
            is also a possible value of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p
            The philosophy behind this sort of impredicative definition is that any inhabitant of such a type must be defined “uniformly” enough that it uses no details about the type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            the identity
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi α
                    %mo →
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id\colon \alpha\to\alpha
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∀
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi Type
                    %mo ,
                    %mi α
                    %mo →
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \forall \alpha:Type, \alpha\to\alpha
          %p
            This sort of impredicativism can be shown to be incompatible with impredicative set-theoretic axioms such as power sets; see
            %a{:href => "https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~amp12/papers/nontpt/nontpt.pdf"} this paper
            of Andy Pitts. Since such type theories generally do have function types, it follows that they cannot be classical.
          %p
            However, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            as talked about in the previous paragraphs usually have non-propositional types. There do exist
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universes"} type universes
            which have impredicative polymorphism and are consistent with power sets: these are the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universes+of+propositions"} universes of propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            where every type is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mere+proposition"} mere proposition
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+polymorphism"} impredicative polymorphism
            says that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            is closed under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicates"} predicates
            valued in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
          %p
            In particular, the condition of having a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+of+all+propositions"} universe of all propositions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}
            is exactly that of having
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            in the type theory, and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Prop
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Prop}
            has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+polymorphism"} impredicative polymorphism
            if and only if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+function+extensionality"} weak function extensionality
            holds, which is equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+extensionality"} function extensionality
          %h2#the_category_of_sets The category of sets
          %p So what is the category of sets in predicative mathematics?
          %p
            At bottom, let us suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heyting+category"} Heyting
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretopos"} pretopos
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+logic"} internal logic
            is first-order and contains only constructions that don't require any of the above axioms.
          %p
            Since we're not doing finite mathematics, we may also include a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+object"} natural numbers object
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inductive+type"} inductive type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            does rely on possibly impredicative axioms. Then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is a Heyting
            = succeed "-pretopos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            If you accept function sets, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+cartesian+closed+category"} locally cartesian closed
            and thus a
            = succeed "-pretopos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} mathb
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathb{N}
            is enough to get all
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-type"} types
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "-pretopos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Boolean+category"} Boolean
            pretopos or even a Boolean
            = succeed "-pretopos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "-pretopos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
          %p
            However,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            is still a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topos"} Grothendieck topos
          %h2#the_real_numbers The real numbers
          %p
            An important question in predicative mathematics is the status of the set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_D
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ×
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \times \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})
            or as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subquotient"} subquotient
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_C
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℕ
                      %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}^{\mathbb{N}}
            = succeed "-pretopos." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "-pretopos," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
          %p
            The constructive school of predicativism can construct
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            in various ways. One method is to use
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_C
            directly, but this will only go so far unless something is done to prove that it is Dedekind-complete. This will follow from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+countable+choice"} weak countable choice
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi WCC
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} WCC
            ), which is accepted by some constructive schools. Using
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset+collection"} subset collection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_C
            is possible which can be proved Dedekind-complete
            %em without
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi WCC
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} WCC
          %p
            However, not all constructive predicative mathematics accept
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset+collection"} subset collection
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+countable+choice"} weak countable choice
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi R
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-frame"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              \-frame
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dedekind+cuts"} Dedekind cuts
            are defined as pairs of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L, U
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+numbers"} rational numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℚ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_D
            is defined as the set of all
            = succeed "-Dedekind" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sigma-Dedekind+complete"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Sigma
              \-Dedekind complete
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/archimedean+field"} archimedean field
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+analysis"} real analysis
            could be developed using this approach, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+calculus"} differential calculus
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+calculus"} integral calculus
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
          %p
            It is also possible to assert the existence of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            by fiat, much like
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            exists by the axiom of infinity. This is the approach taken by the classical school; they use
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}\mathbb{N}
            instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            directly, but these are isomorphic by excluded middle. This is natural from the perspective of predicative set theory as a weak form of higher-order logic; you assert the existence of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}\mathbb{N}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi 𝒫
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}\mathbb{N}
          %p
            One could also assert
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            by fiat via the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of the real numbers as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+field"} Archimedean ordered field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+coalgebra"} terminal coalgebra
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+functor"} identity endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto X
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+fields"} Archimedean ordered fields
          %p
            If
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient+sets"} quotient sets
            exist and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequence+algebras"} sequence algebras
            of Archimedean ordered fields exist, then it is provable that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is Cauchy complete. From the definition of terminal object,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is an algebra of the endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \mathcal{C}(X)
            in the category of Archimedean ordered fields which takes Archimedean ordered fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the Archimedean ordered field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(X)
            of equivalence classes of Cauchy sequences in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \mathcal{C}(X)
            in the category of Archimedean ordered fields is a Cauchy complete Archimedean ordered field.
          %p
            Similarly, if
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            of Archimedean ordered fields exist, then it is provable that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is Dedekind complete. From the definition of terminal object,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            is an algebra of the endofunctor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \mathcal{D}(X)
            in the category of Archimedean ordered fields which takes Archimedean ordered fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            to the Archimedean ordered field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}(X)
            of two-sided Dedekind cuts in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi 𝒟
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \mathcal{D}(X)
            in the category of Archimedean ordered fields is a Dedekind complete Archimedean ordered field.
          %p
            There is also the question of what exactly it means to say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            exists; is it a set or a
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+class"} proper class
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+category"} small
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe+in+a+topos"} universe in a topos
            for impredicative set theory. If you allow
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            only as a proper class, then you are doing predicative mathematics so long as you don’t have
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+classes"} power classes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            as a set, then as long as you don’t allow all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+sets"} power sets
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/power+classes"} power classes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limited+principle+of+omniscience"} limited principle of omniscience
            holds but not full
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excluded+middle"} excluded middle
            is still constructive mathematics.
          %h2#formalising_mathematics Formalising mathematics
          %p How much of mathematics can be done predicatively?
          %p
            A surprisingly large amount of mathematics can be formalised, using various coding tricks, in a theory in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
            is a set but
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}\mathbb{N}
            is a proper class. This is somewhat easier in
            = succeed "'s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nik+Weaver"} Nik Weaver
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi ℕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}\mathcal{P}\mathbb{N}
            as a proper class (so that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}N
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            are small); the encoding is not really more complicated than what is usually done in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
            for ordered pairs and the like. Note that these are conservative over
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano+arithmetic"} Peano arithmetic
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PA
            ); that is, anything expressible in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PA
            and provable in these systems is provable in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PA
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PA
            (which certainly cannot be said of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ZFC"} ZFC
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ETCS"} ETCS
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PA
          %p
            Constructive mathematics generally requires great care with anything after the middle of the 19th century other than basic
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+mathematics"} discrete mathematics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi
            = succeed "-pretopos" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PA
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PA
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+topos"} predicative topos
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/impredicative+dependent+type+theory"} impredicative dependent type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+predicative+dependent+type+theory"} strongly predicative dependent type theory
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Solomon+Feferman"}> Solomon Feferman
                ;
                %a{:href => "http://hlombardi.free.fr/FefermanRelationships.pdf"} Relationships between Constructive, Predicative and Classical Systems of Analysis
                (PDF).
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nik+Weaver"}> Nik Weaver
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "https://web.archive.org/web/20100616093000/https://www.math.wustl.edu/~nweaver/conceptualism.html"} papers on conceptualism
            %li
              %p from the Standford Encyclopedia of Philosophy:
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/set-theory-constructive/#PreConSetThe"}> Predicativity in constructive set theory
                  \.
                %li
                  %a{:href => "http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/philosophy-mathematics/index.html#Pre"} Predicativism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nik+Weaver"}> Nik Weaver
                ,
                %em Predicativity beyond Gamma_0
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0509244"}> arXiv:math/0509244
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nik+Weaver"}> Nik Weaver
                ,
                %em Predicative well-ordering
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.03543"}> arXiv:1811.03543
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nik+Weaver"}> Nik Weaver
                ,
                %em Hierarchies of Tarskian truth predicates
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.00851"}> arXiv:2202.00851
                )
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicative+toposes"} predicative toposes
            is in
          %ul#vdBerg
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ieke+Moerdijk"}> Ieke Moerdijk
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Erik+Palmgren"} Erik Palmgren
                %em
                  Type theories, toposes and constructive set theory: predicative aspects of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+set+theory"} algebraic set theory
                Ann. Pure Appl. Logic, 114(1-3):155–201, 2002
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Benno+van+den+Berg"}> Benno van den Berg
                ,
                %em Predicative toposes
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1207.0959"}> arXiv:1207.0959
                )
          %p Constructive predicative definitions of the real numbers are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Taylor"}> Paul Taylor
                's
                %a{:href => "http://www.paultaylor.eu/ASD/dedras/classical"} page on Dedekind cuts
            %li
              %p
                Univalent Foundations Project,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/HoTT+book"} Homotopy Type Theory – Univalent Foundations of Mathematics
                (2013)
